We tested whether retaining more of the original instruction model would help a trained two-pass decoder respond to a change in meaning or operate on a real file. Against untouched ordinary-depth Base, the loop answered *17/40 versus 18/40* original Winograd questions and solved both halves of *0/20 versus 1/20* meaning pairs. Each system grounded one of sixteen file requests; neither completed a two-request file task. The loop generated 10,800 tokens versus 25,252 and took *114.63 versus 370.32 seconds*. Its first-token waits summed to 6.93 versus 6.62 seconds. The primary gate failed. This policy stays experimental. The separate 128K-capable release and matched induction result are documented in the main report.
